with attribution to Meta Platforms, Inc. They are reproduced rather than called
because the released checkpoint was trained against a locally modified copy of
that method, and monkeypatching the vendored version back over the pinned
submodule is what preserves numerical parity with the released weights.


Discrete Elastic Rods (DER) — formulation attribution
-----------------------------------------------------
The elastic-rod losses in src/dlofeel_state/losses/ (der.py, bending.py)
implement the Discrete Elastic Rods formulation of Bergou, Wardetzky, Robinson,
Audoly, and Grinspun, "Discrete elastic rods," ACM Transactions on Graphics
27(3), 2008 (doi:10.1145/1360612.1360662). This is an independent
reimplementation of that published formulation. Its structure was informed by
the reference implementation in roahmlab/DEFORM (Chen et al., "Differentiable
Discrete Elastic Rods for Real-Time Modeling of Deformable Linear Objects,"
CoRL 2024, arXiv:2406.05931).

The Theseus-based nonlinear twist solver from DEFORM is deliberately NOT
reproduced here, and this repository has no theseus dependency. In its place,
losses/der.py provides a self-contained twist entry point,
optimize_twist_angle(), which performs no solve: it returns a zero twist angle
(twist-free rod). It is called only from pipeline_points_to_material_frame() in
the same file, which in turn is reached only from the optional bending-energy
path in losses/bending.py (and from each file's own __main__ demo block). The
shipped training loss, losses/weighted.py::WeightedLoss, never enters that path:
it imports only compute_edges, compute_edge_lengths, and
compute_curvature_binormal from losses/der.py, so no twist or material-frame
code participates in the released training or evaluation objective.

roahmlab/DEFORM publishes no license, so no DEFORM code is copied or
redistributed; it is credited here only as prior art that guided this
independent implementation.


torchcurves — Apache-2.0, pip dependency
----------------------------------------
Differentiable B-spline curve fitting uses torchcurves (alexshtf/torchcurves),
licensed Apache-2.0, consumed as an ordinary PyPI dependency (torchcurves>=0.2.1)
and not redistributed in this repository. Apache-2.0 is compatible with MIT and
imposes no additional obligation on this repository beyond those already met by
declaring it as a dependency.
